            "<strong>External beam radiotherapy</strong> is painless and makes nobody radioactive, which families need to hear; its effects are on the field and on the patient's energy. The <strong>skin in the field</strong> is washed gently with lukewarm water and mild unscented soap and patted dry, with <strong>no rubbing, no perfume, no deodorant, no powder, no adhesive tape, no hot-water bottles or ice, no sun</strong> and loose cotton clothing; the <strong>marks are never washed off</strong>; moisturiser is used only as the unit allows; and dry or moist desquamation is reported and dressed as directed. Radiotherapy to the head and neck causes <strong>mucositis</strong>, a dry mouth, taste loss and dental problems, so the teeth are seen before treatment and mouth care is hourly; to the chest, oesophagitis; to the abdomen and pelvis, nausea, <strong>diarrhoea</strong>, cystitis and later fibrosis; everywhere, <strong>fatigue</strong> that builds over the weeks and is met with planned rest and gentle activity, not bed. <strong>Brachytherapy</strong> — a sealed source placed in the cervix or the breast for hours or days — makes the <strong>patient</strong> a source for that time: a single room, <strong>time, distance and shielding</strong> for staff, no pregnant staff or children visiting, care given quickly from the foot of the bed, the patient kept flat with a catheter and a low-residue diet so that the applicator does not move, and a <strong>dislodged source never touched with the hands</strong> but lifted with long forceps into the lead container while the physicist is called.",
