            "<strong>Palliative care</strong> is the active total care of a person whose illness will not be cured, aimed at the best quality of life for her and her family, and it <strong>begins at diagnosis and runs alongside curative treatment</strong> — not after the surgeon has given up — for cancer, HIV, heart and kidney failure, stroke, sickle cell disease, dementia and the dying child; <strong>hospice</strong> care is the part of it focused on comfort in the last months when cure is no longer the goal. All hospice care is palliative; not all palliative care is hospice. Its organising idea is <strong>total pain</strong>, which has four dimensions — <strong>physical, psychological, social and spiritual</strong> — and morphine alone will not settle a pain that is really fear, debt, a quarrel with a brother or unfinished business with God; the nurse assesses all four and the team (nurse, doctor, social worker, chaplain or imam, family) treats all four, in the hospital, in the hospice at Korle Bu and the few others, and increasingly <strong>at home</strong>, where most Ghanaians would choose to die and where the community nurse and a trained family are the service.",
            "<strong>Physical pain</strong> is assessed like any other — site, character, severity on a scale, what relieves and worsens it, its effect on sleep and movement, the patient's own word for it — and treated by the <strong>WHO analgesic ladder</strong> with its five principles: <strong>by mouth</strong> (injections are for those who cannot swallow), <strong>by the clock</strong> (regular doses before the pain returns), <strong>by the ladder</strong> (step 1 paracetamol and a non-steroidal; step 2 a weak opioid such as codeine or tramadol added; step 3 <strong>morphine</strong>, with the step 1 drugs continued throughout), <strong>for the individual</strong> (the right dose is the one that relieves her pain), and <strong>with attention to detail</strong> (written instructions, side effects anticipated, review). <strong>Oral morphine</strong> solution is started at a small dose — 2.5–5 mg every four hours in the frail and 5–10 mg in others — with a <strong>breakthrough dose of about one-sixth of the daily total</strong> available whenever needed, the regular dose increased by a third to a half each day until the pain is controlled, a double dose at bedtime to allow sleep, and conversion to slow-release tablets when the daily need is known; <strong>there is no ceiling dose</strong> — the correct dose is the one that works. Its side effects are prescribed for in advance: <strong>constipation is certain and never wears off</strong>, so a stimulant laxative starts with the first dose; nausea in the first days has an antiemetic; drowsiness passes within days; a dry mouth needs mouth care. The nurse reads a patient on an opioid by the <strong>level of sedation first and the respiratory rate second</strong>, because sedation deepens before breathing slows; a rousable patient with a normal rate whose pain is controlled is exactly where you want to be. The myths that kill: opioids titrated against pain in a dying patient <strong>do not shorten life</strong>, <strong>addiction is not a risk that matters</strong> here, and the common error on Ghanaian wards is not overdose but families and staff <strong>withholding doses out of fear</strong> while the patient dies in pain that was entirely treatable. <strong>Adjuvants</strong> join the ladder at any step: amitriptyline or gabapentin for the burning, shooting pain of nerve damage, dexamethasone for the pressure of a swelling tumour or a liver capsule, a bisphosphonate and a single fraction of radiotherapy for bone pain, hyoscine for colic, a nerve block for the pain nothing else reaches. And because morphine is both genuinely needed and genuinely diverted, the <strong>controlled-drug chain never breaks</strong>: a locked cupboard with one key-holder per shift, a register with a running balance, two nurses checking and both signing, a count at every shift change, waste witnessed and recorded, and a <strong>discrepancy reported at once, in writing, before leaving the ward</strong> — never adjusted to fit the shelf, never left for the next shift, never quietly replaced — because delay is what turns an error into a suspicion.",
