        "Palliative care starts at diagnosis alongside treatment for any life-limiting illness and hospice is its comfort-focused subset; total pain is physical, psychological, social and spiritual; the WHO ladder is by mouth, by the clock, by the ladder, for the individual, with attention to detail — paracetamol and NSAID, then a weak opioid, then oral morphine started at 2.5–10 mg four-hourly with a breakthrough dose of one-sixth of the daily total, titrated daily, doubled at night, with no ceiling, a laxative from the first dose, sedation watched before respiratory rate, adjuvants for nerve, bone and pressure pain, and the controlled-drug chain kept unbroken with any discrepancy reported in writing at once.",
        "Treat nausea by mechanism, breathlessness with a fan, position, presence, low-dose morphine and oxygen only if hypoxic, the mouth four-hourly, confusion by its reversible cause; in the last days stop what is unnecessary, give essential drugs subcutaneously, keep the mouth moist, keep the family present and informed; after death ask about washing and rites before proceeding, respect loud communal grief, explain the mortuary steps and write them down, remember the child's family and the siblings, and debrief the team.",
